  "stable_key": "n7-lesson-08",
  "position": 4,
  "title": "Simultaneous linear equations",
  "summary": "Solve pairs of linear equations by substitution, elimination and graphs, then check the common solution.",
  "outcomes": [
    "7.8"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "n7-lesson-07"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 60,
  "objectives": [
    "Choose between substitution and elimination efficiently.",
    "Interpret the intersection of two lines as a simultaneous solution.",
    "Verify a solution in both original equations."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-explanation-1",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "One pair, one common solution",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A solution of simultaneous equations must satisfy both equations at the same time. Algebraically, substitution or elimination reduces the pair to one variable. Graphically, the solution is the point where the two straight lines intersect. The diagram shows the lines of the second worked example, x + y = 8 and 4x + 3y = 29, crossing at (5, 3)."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n7-lesson-08-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-explanation-2",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Choose substitution or elimination",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Substitution is efficient when one variable is already isolated or has coefficient 1. Elimination is efficient when coefficients match or become opposites after a small multiplication. Record any multiplication across every term of an equation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-explanation-3",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Use graphs with the right precision",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Plot at least two accurate points for each line and read the intersection using the stated scale. A graph may give an estimate, so substitute the read coordinates into both equations when an exact answer is expected. Parallel distinct lines have no common solution; the same line has infinitely many."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-errors",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When multiplying an equation to match coefficients, multiply every term, including the constant. Subtract whole equations carefully, sign by sign. Find both unknowns, not just one, and check the pair in both original equations, because a pair that satisfies only one equation is not a solution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-example-1",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: eliminate directly",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Solve 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The y-terms are opposites, so add the equations to eliminate y."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "3x=12\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=4"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute into x − y = 1."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "4-y=1\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y=3"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "(4, 3) satisfies both: 8 + 3 = 11 and 4 − 3 = 1."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-example-2",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: use substitution",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Solve x + y = 8 and 4x + 3y = 29."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "From x + y = 8, write y in terms of x."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=8-x"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute into the second equation and simplify."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "4x+3(8-x)=29\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 4x+24-3x=29\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=8-5=3"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "(5, 3) gives 5 + 3 = 8 and 20 + 9 = 29, and it is the crossing point in the diagram."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For 2x + y = 11 and x + y = 7, find x."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "4",
        "canonical_latex": "4",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Subtracting the second equation from the first gives x = 4 (and then y = 3)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n7-08-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The solution of a pair of simultaneous equations satisfies both equations; on a graph it is the point where the two lines cross."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use substitution when a variable is easy to isolate, and elimination when coefficients match or can be matched. Find both values and check them in both original equations."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
